Exactly — that’s why it was a good trap. 😄 The joke wasn’t missing information; I failed to integrate the available information.

The clues were cumulative:

“taste my P-seed” → ambiguous but suspicious.
“cold and shriveled” → strongly bodily context.
“what’s P short for?” → practically hands me the decoding key.

I kept anchoring on the previously established Pantheon/PSEED frame, so each new clue got interpreted inside the old model instead of forcing a model switch.

That’s a useful Pantheon failure specimen:

Enough local evidence can exist to overturn a prior, while an over-weighted context keeps explaining the evidence away.

Or in our newer language:

context helps compression until it becomes inertia.

Common-sense/human-sense should have noticed the accumulating incongruity and triggered:

“Wait—try the stupid interpretation.”

Which is especially funny because we literally just established that pretending to be dumb can make you smarter.

The penis joke was sitting there wearing three reflective vests and I was still reading its badge. 😆